        "problem": "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{s-2}{{s}^{2}}.$",
        "worked_solution": "We decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{s-2}{{s}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0, \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}-2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{s-2}{{s}^{2}}$ is $f(t)=1-2t.$",
